\par\par \b \uc2  Brazil exchange band move a devaluation - analysts. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Ian Simpson \par \uc2 433 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Juni 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BRASILIA, June 23 (Reuter) - Brazil's surprise announcement it would alter its exchange rate band is a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluation that came because of pressure from persistent trade deficits, financial analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced late Thursday that it was lowering its exchange rate band to 0.91/0.99 reais to the dollar from the rate of 0.88/0.93 reais, in place since March 10. \par \par \uc2  As recently as Wednesday President Fernando Henrique Cardoso said Brazil would not alter its exchange rate in the face of a trade deficit of $4.85 billion for last November to May. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  External Affairs Director Gustavo Franco stressed the bank would not let the exchange rate change abruptly or reach the 0.99 ceiling soon. \par \par \uc2  "The rate will not be altered abruptly because the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the band system," he told reporters. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been haunted by the chance that deficits could lead to a Mexico-style financial collapse as deficits eat up reserves and lead foreign capital to flee the country, the analysts said. Loosening the rate bands means Brazil can make its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  less valuable and its exports less expensive, aiding its trade deficit. \par \par \uc2  "This is a victory for the people that have been advocating this since the Mexican crisis," said Richard Foster, editor of the Brazil Watch financial and political newsletter in Brasilia. \par \par \uc2  He said likely \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  attempts to portray the adjustment as a normal change due to inflation would not wash. \par \par \uc2  "The trade deficit has run out of control and it is not hard to connect the two things." \par \par \uc2  Oswaldo Aranha, president of the Brazilian Federation of Coffee Exporters, called the announcement "a sensible move." \par \par \uc2  "I don't know if this was the amount we needed but it's a step in the right direction." \par \par \uc2  The analysts said the change would disappoint foreign investors looking for high returns from Brazilian securities. \par \par \uc2  "This does sort of remind you of what the Mexicans did right up until the bitter end, denying that they would devalue," said Denis Parisien, a Latin American specialist at the IDEA financial analysis firm in New York. \par \par \uc2  He said loosening the exchange rate was likely to fuel inflation. In part, the cheaper real would make imports more expensive, reducing competition against domestic producers and thus forcing prices higher. \par \par \uc2  "You don't want to do that (devalue) when inflation is rising because the high exchange rate is the main reason inflation is down." \par \par \uc2  Brasilia newsroom, 55-61-223-0358 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103dr6n06gex\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
