\par\par \b \uc2  Brazil/ \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange \highlight\uc2  -2: Policy Makes Imports Cheaper \b0\par\par\uc2 174 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Februar 1995\par 15:08\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Co., Inc.) \par \par \uc2  The story couldn't be confirmed immediately through the president's office. \par \par \uc2  Brazil introduced its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the real, last July 1 on par to the U.S. dollar. Since then, the dollar has declined to 83 centavos and inflation, which has been running at more than 80%, hasn't been compensated for the real in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. Economists say these two factors together have made the real overvalued by about 40%. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  by spending as much as 1 billion dlrs on a single day to keep the real at the current exchange level. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy has made imports inexpensive, forcing Brazilian producers to keep their prices low. Economists predict that if the real is allowed to depreciate, imports would become more expensive and domestic manufacturers would raise their prices as well. The government's top priority is fighting inflation, which was 0.8% in January, down from 50% last June. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 09-02-95 \par \par \uc2  1408GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011025dr2900uqr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
