\par\par \b \uc2  Brazil  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Puts Bank Under 'Special Administration' \b0\par\par\uc2 262 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Februar 1995\par 19:55\par \uc2  Dow Jones News Service \par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Co., Inc.) \par \par \uc2  BRASILIA -DJ- \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Bemat, the bank owned by the state of Mato Grosso, placing it under special temporary administration. \par \par \uc2  In a statement, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Bemat because it ''failed to honor its commitments.'' Bemat owed Brazil's National Treasury 364 million reals, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said its objective is to put Bemat in a healthy condition during the temporary administration ''as a means of making it possible to transfer control of its shares.'' \par \par \uc2  A \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said recently that privatization is the best solution for the country's troubled state banks. \par \par \uc2  Bemat is the fourth state bank to be placed under temporary \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  administration since the end of last year, following the state banks in Sao Paulo, Rio de Janeiro and Alagoas. A bank owned by the state of Rio Grande do Norte was closed by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Dec. 29, 1994. \par \par \uc2  Many of Brazil's state-owned banks have been in financial difficulty since the introduction of an inflation-beating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the real, on July 1, 1994. \par \par \uc2  In its statement, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Bemat's management has been guilty of ''hardly recommendable'' practices since the 1980s, but that its losses were hidden by gains from the free ''float'' provided by high inflation. Brazil's monthly inflation reached 50% in June last year before dropping to current levels of 1% to 2% after the introduction of the real. \par \par \uc2  (END) DOW JONES NEWS 02-02-95 \par \par \uc2  1:55 PM \par \par \uc2 Dokument dj00000020011024dr2200jw2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
